  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry, tranquil and increasingly milder into the \nweekend \n\nMultiday stretch of sunny and increasingly warmer days continues\ninto the weekend. Fcst Max temps peak 10-20 degrees above the \nhistorical average Thu-Sun while nights will run closer to climo\nresulting in large diurnal ranges indicative of a prolonged \nlate Autumn dry spell. Expect ideal conditions for outdoor pre \nwinter activities: raking leaves, set up holiday decorations, \nor enjoying time outside, it will be an very pleasant stretch of\nweather for the 3rd week of November. \n\nLeaning colder for overnight low temps through midweek given \nfavorable radiational cooling setup and below normal PWAT under\ndome of high pressure. This pattern will also promote late \nnight and early morning valley fog formation. Freezing fog and\nslick spots are possible where road temps can drop below 32F. \n\nContinued to trim dewpoints through Thursday with minRH \nbottoming in the 20-30% range. Despite the low humidity and \ndrying fuels, light wind speeds will limit potential wildfire \nspread. \n\nA dry spell is expected to last into the weekend. Recent model\nand AI runs (particularly the GFS/AIGFS) show the potential for\nlow pressure developing off the FL coast to track up the \nEastern Seaboard bringing rain to CPA on Sunday. A weakening \ncold front moving eastward from the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley \nmay also produce some rain later in the weekend. Latest NBM/WPC\nforecast largely downplays both rain making systems at this \npoint given the various solution spread, perpetuating a mainly \ndry forecast into early next week.",
